1. Usability Testing Design
My goal is to make sure the "Car Search" is actually easy for a human to use, not just functional.

What I would look at:
Can a user find their car in under 3 clicks?
If they click "Search," is it obvious that the page is loading, or does it look frozen?
Helpfulness: If they search for something that doesn't exist, does the site give them a "No results found" message with suggestions, or just a blank white screen?

Test Cases:
Check if typing "VW" brings up "Volkswagen" (smart search).
Make sure filters work together (e.g., searching for a "2022" "Audi" shouldn't show a "2010" "BMW").
Testing on a mobile screen to ensure buttons aren't too small to tap.

2. Performance Testing Strategy
I want to make sure the site doesn't crash when 500 people try to search for cars at the same time.

Tools: I would use k6 to simulate a crowd of users hitting the search button.
Stress Test: I'll keep adding "virtual users" until the site starts to slow down or crash. This tells us exactly how much traffic we can handle.

Success Criteria:
Speed: Search results should show up in under 2 seconds.
Stability: Even under heavy load, the site shouldn't throw "404" or "500" errors.

3. Client Collaboration
I would keep the business team in the loop throughout.

How I would involve client:
Before I start, I would ask the client which car brands are their top sellers so I can prioritize testing those specific search paths.

Instead of sending a massive technical spreadsheet, I will send a "traffic light" report (Green = Good, Yellow = Slow, Red = Fix immediately).

If the client says "our users are mostly on old phones," I wll pivot my testing to focus more on mobile performance rather than high-end desktops.
